@attribute [Route(GlobalValues.Path_Theming_Overview)]

<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.Page_Title_Theming_Overview</PageTitle>

<section class="info-box info-box--coloured">

<h1>@GlobalValues.Page_Heading_Theming_Overview</h1>

<p>
    This section of the documentation provides you with all of the necessary details to be able to style any or all components to meet your needs.
</p>
<p>
    Although Blazor Ramp makes no claims about being a CSS framework or even offering a theming service, once you understand how it works, it is possible to use it as such.
</p>
<p>
    For example, if you are starting a fresh project without reliance on other CSS and/or component frameworks where you need Blazor Ramp components to blend in, you could build 
    your own components following the same logic and hierarchical nature using the provided CSS variables, so that the mix of your own and Blazor Ramp components would be seamlessly 
    styled as one.
</p>
</section>

<section class="section-box">

    <h2>How theming works in Blazor Ramp</h2>
    <p>
        Blazor Ramp has three categories of CSS variables, the first two of which are shared by everything.
    </p>
    
    <section>
        <h3>Units (<span class="code-span code-span--h3-sizing">--br-unit</span>)</h3>
        <p>
            These define all of the base unitary values such as widths, spaces, fonts and colours, and are the underlying values which every variable ultimately uses unless overridden 
            by an individual component.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h3>All components (<span class="code-span code-span--h3-sizing">--br-comp-all</span>)</h3>
        <p>
            These form the base set of variables that all components use unless overridden by the individual component.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h3>Component specific</h3>
        <p>
            These are defined on each individual component. 
            For example, those defined for the Announcement History component are prefixed <span class="code-span">--br-comp-history</span>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Unless it was necessary to do so for the component, each component-specific variable will map to a shared <span class="code-span">--br-comp-all</span> variable. 
            For example, the Announcement History outline that appears around the dialogue on focus has the variable <span class="code-span">--br-comp-history-outline-style</span>,
            which maps to (has the value) <span class="code-span">var(--br-comp-all-outline-style)</span>.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h3>Component Classes</h3>
        <p>
            Each component uses the Block Element Modifier (BEM) syntax and as such each component has its own block class, such as <span class="code-span">.br-announcement-history</span>.
        </p>
        <p>
            The styles set in the classes map to the component-specific variables unless overridden in the class. For example, the dialogue's background property 
            maps to <span class="code-span">var(--br-comp-history-pane-base-background)</span>.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h3>Hierarchy of granularity</h3>
        <p>The hierarchy of granularity is as follows:</p>
        <ol style="margin-left:var(--br-unit-space-1);margin-bottom:var(--br-unit-space-5);">
            <li>
                <span class="code-span">--br-unit</span> - changes everything everywhere.
            </li>
            <li>
                <span class="code-span">--br-comp-all</span> - changes the parts of all components that map to this variable.
            </li>
            <li>
                <span class="code-span">--br-comp-[component-identifier]</span> - changes the value that is used for the style property it's mapped to.
            </li>
            <li>
                <span class="code-span">.br-[class-identifier]</span> - modifies the style properties of the component directly.
            </li>
        </ol>

    </section>

    <section>
        <h3>The concept</h3>
        <p>
            The concept is very simple, but there are a lot of variables, so it can appear daunting. However, most variables will just map back to the units, so changing a single unit 
            colour value will ripple through the entire system.
            The process is generally simple: <b>start at the top to change everything and only change things at the bottom where necessary.</b>
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h3>Example</h3>
        <p>
            The difference between this documentation site and the <a target="_blank" class="@GlobalValues.Link_Class" href="@GlobalValues.Web_Site_Path_Component_Test">component test site (opens in new window)</a> was achieved 
            by changing the <span class="code-span">--br-unit-colour-primary</span> from <span class="code-span">hsl(220, 44%, 48%)</span> to the named colour <span class="code-span">Sienna</span>. I implemented a 
            very rudimentary colour theming system where the secondary, accent and neutral colours were all derived from the primary colour using just CSS colour functions, without SASS (which I normally use).
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h3>Integration with your framework</h3>
        <p>
            As you will most likely be using your own framework or a third party's, you will most likely need to change more than a single value to have any component that you choose to use blend in with your existing components.
            But this is a one time simple process.
        </p>
    </section>

</section>

